Before beginning enumeration, significant time was spent troubleshooting the lab environment. The issue was not with Kioptrix itself but with the virtual networking configuration.

Problem 1: Kioptrix Failed to Boot Correctly

After importing the VM, Kioptrix repeatedly failed to obtain network information and sometimes became stuck during boot. Investigation showed that the original VM configuration expected an older VMware hardware configuration and networking setup.

Observation

The original .vmx file contained:

ethernet0.networkName = "Bridged"

which caused VMware to repeatedly use Bridged networking instead of the desired Host-only configuration.

Resolution

The VM was rebuilt using the original VMDK disk while manually configuring the network adapter.

Problem 2: Kernel Panic During Boot

During one attempt, the VM produced:

Kernel panic - No init found

This occurred because the virtual disk was not attached correctly.

Resolution

The original Kioptrix VMDK file was attached as the primary hard disk, allowing the operating system to locate its root filesystem and boot successfully.

Problem 3: Hardware Discovery Prompts

After successful booting, Kioptrix displayed several hardware detection messages such as:

Ensoniq ES1371 AudioPCI
Intel PIIX4 USB Controller

These messages appeared because VMware presented virtual hardware not present in the original machine.

Resolution

The devices were ignored since they were unnecessary for the penetration testing exercise.

Problem 4: Network Discovery Failed

Initially, running:

netdiscover

or

arp-scan

failed to locate the Kioptrix host.

Investigation

Using:

ip addr

revealed that Kali had multiple interfaces:

eth0
eth1

Only one interface had received an IP address.

Further examination with:

nmcli device status

showed:

eth1 connected
eth0 disconnected

This indicated that NetworkManager had assigned the active connection to the Host-only interface.

Problem 5: No Internet Connectivity

Although Kali could communicate with the lab network, internet access was unavailable.

Investigation

Checking routes:

ip route

showed only:

192.168.177.0/24 dev eth1

No default gateway existed.

As a result:

ping 8.8.8.8

returned:

Network is unreachable
Resolution

The NAT adapter was reconnected and assigned correctly, restoring internet connectivity while maintaining access to the Host-only lab network.

Final Network Configuration

The final working configuration was:

Kali
eth0 -> NAT
eth1 -> Host-only
Kioptrix
eth0 -> Host-only

This created the following topology:

Internet
   |
 NAT
   |
 Kali (eth0)

Host-only Network
   |
 Kali (eth1) <----> Kioptrix
Host Discovery

Once the network was functioning correctly, host discovery was performed using:

arp-scan -I eth1 -l

The scan identified the target:

192.168.177.129

Connectivity was verified using:

ping -c 4 192.168.177.129

which returned successful ICMP replies.
